/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  userOp03_case_rate_law  --  ONE rate law, written TWICE, by the two routes
  a case has to carry its own kinetics (DEV.md 4c C67).

    reactorCode   reads `kinetics { type michaelisMenten; }` -- a law
                  written in C++ in THIS case's code/ folder and registered
                  into the engine's rate-law factory from
                  code/registerUserTypes.cpp.  `bin/buildCode` compiles it
                  into a per-case binary; it runs on a machine with a
                  compiler and NOT on the site (the browser has none).

    reactorExpr   reads `kinetics { type expression; }` -- the SAME law
                  written as a formula in constant/reactions, evaluated by
                  the engine's own expression evaluator.  It runs
                  everywhere, the site included.

  The reaction is an ISOMERISATION, isomerA -> isomerB, between two
  synthetic species this case carries in its own constant/components/: one
  molar mass between them, so each reactor's mass balance closes at 100 %
  (the witness used to turn the catalogue's compA, MW 30, into compB, MW 50,
  one-for-one, and the engine reported 133.333 % -- correctly), and one molar
  liquid volume, so the constant-volumetric-flow liquid basis is exact.

  The two reactors are fed the same stream and must give the same answer;
  both must equal the closed-form CSTR solution of Michaelis-Menten
  kinetics (a quadratic in the outlet concentration).  `bin/curate/
  check_rate_expression.py` holds all three together.

  Why ever write the C++ one, if the formula is shorter?  Because some laws
  are not a formula: a rate that needs an inner solve (a surface coverage
  found by Newton, a population moment), a loop, a table the case reads
  from a file.  The C++ route is the door for those; this witness walks
  through it with a law simple enough to check by hand.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        reactorCode;
        type        cstr;
        in          feedCode;
        outputs     ( productCode );
        operation   { V_R 0.025; }               // m^3
        reactions   ( mm_code );                 // law: code/MichaelisMenten.{H,cpp}
    }
    {
        name        reactorExpr;
        type        cstr;
        in          feedExpr;
        outputs     ( productExpr );
        operation   { V_R 0.025; }               // m^3
        reactions   ( mm_expr );                 // law: constant/reactions, `type expression`
    }
);
